## The application — AGPL-3.0-or-later

Everything except `packages/calendar` is under the GNU Affero General Public
License, version 3 or later. The full text is in LICENSE.

Why AGPL rather than GPL: this is self-hosted software, and the obvious way to
take it without giving anything back is to run a modified copy as a service.
The AGPL's network clause is the whole reason it was chosen. Running it
unmodified for your own household — which is what it is for — carries no
obligation at all. You do not have to publish anything, register anything, or
tell anybody.

## The ICS engine — MIT

`packages/calendar` is under the MIT License; its text is in
packages/calendar/LICENSE.

It parses iCalendar and expands recurrence, and it is deliberately free of
anything specific to this product: no I/O, no vendor SDK, no framework. That is
so it can be lifted out into its own repository and used by anybody, including
in software that would never be able to take the AGPL. Correct RFC 5545
handling is scarce enough that keeping it behind a copyleft licence would help
nobody.

If you take one thing from this repository, take that.

## Third-party

Dependencies keep their own licences. `pnpm licenses list` will print them, and
the container image ships a software bill of materials alongside it.

A few assets are copied into the repository rather than depended on, so that
list will not name them: the bundled typefaces (SIL OFL 1.1 and Apache-2.0),
the admin's inline Lucide icons (© Lucide Icons and Contributors, ISC, from
https://github.com/lucide-icons/lucide), and a curated 155-picture subset of
Twemoji (© Twitter, Inc and other contributors, CC-BY 4.0, from
https://github.com/jdecked/twemoji). The typefaces and Lucide are recorded
with their terms in apps/server/assets/fonts/LICENSES.md; the emoji artwork is
recorded in apps/server/assets/emoji/LICENSES.md. Lucide replaced Material
